    {
      "id": "BYtC",
      "code_hash": "5fbb1026ed2b9ea63308ae69467ce9c2",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"interpretation\">Interpretation</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The table and chart show the number of patient visits\nassociated with each clinic. The average visit length\nprovides an additional measure of how long visits\ntypically last at each clinic.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Clinics with more visits have a larger total patient\nvisit volume. The average visit length can be used\nalongside the visit count to understand differences\nin clinic activity.</span>\n<h2 id=\"why-sql-is-useful\">Why SQL Is Useful</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">SQL is useful for this question because the information\nis stored across multiple related tables.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Instead of manually combining the clinic, patient, and\nvisit DataFrames in Python, SQL can join the tables\nusing their keys and calculate the required statistics\nwith COUNT() and AVG().</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">SQL also makes the relationship between the tables\nexplicit and allows the analysis to be performed close\nto the database data before the results are returned\nto Python for visualization.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
